WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.333
How do you make a horror
film that avoids sound?

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:06.500
Let's take a look at the
opening of "A Quiet Place"

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.750
to see how John Krasinski
both establishes a world

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.740
and puts on a master
class in tension building

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.988
with one near-perfect 10-minute sequence.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.386
With just one title card

00:00:25.386 --> 00:00:27.444
and four corresponding shots,

00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:30.410
Krasinski achieves the
first role of an opening:

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.260
establishing the world.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.170
And how "A Quiet Place" achieves this

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.920
is through minimal visual exposition.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.830
Although they might seem pretty random,

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.950
the five opening images actually tell you

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.430
all there is to know about the world.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.580
The title card hints at a major event

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.740
that has occurred before the film,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.370
and the next three
consecutive shots affirm

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.233
and establish a post-apocalyptic world.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.408
In fact, these minimal visual expositions

00:00:57.408 --> 00:00:58.808
are everywhere,

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:00.560
from a child's drawing that hints

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.140
at the creatures' extraterrestrial origins

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.415
to a newspaper

00:01:04.415 --> 00:01:07.820
that explicitly states what the danger is.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.080
Choosing to leave more out than in

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.910
lends the film a sense of mystery

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.490
while offering the bare minimum

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.130
of information to follow the plot.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.790
The second role of an opening is

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.260
identifying the visual language.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.750
Every film has a unique style and tone

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.480
in which it tells a story.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.090
In the case of "A Quiet Place,"

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.473
that languages is "show, don't tell."

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.320
This technique is most evident in

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.580
the opening sequence mentioned prior.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.650
The title card establishes
a specific time frame,

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.250
and the next three
consecutive shots establish

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.460
that law and order have collapsed.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.077
People have abandoned their homes,

00:01:43.077 --> 00:01:45.060
and society is in ruin.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.820
Finally, a wall of
missing people signifies

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.740
that danger is still
lurking in the corners.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.237
Contrary to popular belief,

00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:54.620
"A Quiet Place" does not take inspiration

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.330
from the classic silent films,

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.920
but rather modern visual films.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.543
While silent films that
physically lack sound

00:02:01.543 --> 00:02:04.255
rely on exaggerated visuals, emotions,

00:02:04.255 --> 00:02:06.960
and movements to keep
the audience's attention,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.395
visual films ignore the present sound

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:12.370
to rely on simple, yet
precise shots and edits

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:14.420
to visually convey information.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.090
Krasinski refers to two
main sources of inspiration,

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.470
the Coen brothers' "No
Country for Old Men"

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.430
and Paul Thomas Anderson's
"There Will Be Blood."

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.688
The latter also features

00:02:24.688 --> 00:02:26.546
a 14-minute opening sequence

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:28.810
with no spoken dialogue that can also be

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.830
entirely understood without any sound.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.630
Take a closer look at their
respective screenplays,

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.400
and the similarities
are even more obvious.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.970
Visual films rely on
these long and specific

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.360
scene descriptions that
visualize almost everything,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.060
from the props to the
movement of the characters,

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.230
the tempo of the edits,

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:50.050
and even the direction of the camera.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.432
This unique "show, don't tell" approach

00:02:52.432 --> 00:02:54.690
can be identified everywhere.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.370
Instead of having to explain
the daughter's disability,

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.970
all we need is a single close-up.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:03.440
Want to establish that making
a sound can be dangerous?

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.333
This is all you need to show.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:07.900
This is also the reason why the film

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.010
relies so heavily on reaction shots,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.260
more specifically, characters
reacting to one another.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.910
While a line that clearly expresses

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.660
a character's emotion
might be more convenient,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.150
sometimes a gaze or a change in expression

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.450
can be enough to deliver
it more convincingly.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.610
In fact, the opening is chock-full
of these reaction shots,

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.360
which establish the relationship
between the characters

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.620
and convey a range of emotions,

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.190
like affection, sadness, fear,

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.500
and, ultimately, dread.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.790
Which brings us to the final

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.417
and most important rule of an opening:

00:03:42.417 --> 00:03:44.650
engaging the audience.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.820
In a horror film like "A Quiet Place,"

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.870
that's usually achieved
by scaring the audience.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.570
Except this film has a crucial

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:53.608
limitation in that department.

00:03:57.184 --> 00:03:59.770
[alarm ticking]

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:00.950
Sound.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.241
Take any scene from a horror film...

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.913
and watch it without sound.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.070
And it will most likely
never feel as scary.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.690
To get around this limitation,

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:19.140
Krasinski chooses tension
over an outright scare.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.590
Sometimes it's as simple as showing

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:24.220
who we need to worry about
right from the first image.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.310
After visually associating
sound with danger,

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:30.030
Krasinski delivers three key moments

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:31.940
that gradually build in tension,

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.900
and they are all achieved
in the most brilliant way:

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.900
by not showing themselves,

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.234
whether it's the sister
jumping in out of nowhere

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:41.770
to catch a falling toy at the last second

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:43.750
or the family gripped in fear by something

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.120
the boy is holding that we can't see.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:48.100
Krasinski cleverly uses the film's

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.500
"show, don't tell" approach

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.180
to deliver a unique sort of tension.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.810
Of the entire 10 minutes,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.090
almost a minute and a half is dedicated to

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:57.690
this sort of tension building

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.930
up until the final moment.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:02.130
The boy, whom the audience already knows

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.510
will be the source of trouble,

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.610
is shot only from a
distance or partially hidden

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:08.827
until that dreadful
noise begins offscreen.

00:05:08.827 --> 00:05:10.680
[toy beeping]

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.380
For even heavier impact,

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.250
the reveal happens twice:

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.370
once through the ears of the parents

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:19.060
and then through the
perspective of the mute sister,

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.300
who visually confirms the present danger.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.010
And as the film kills
off the most innocent

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:26.790
and powerless of the bunch,

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.790
the audience is once again reminded

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:30.430
of the gravity of the danger

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.146
and the fact that anyone
can die at any moment.

00:05:33.146 --> 00:05:34.955
[yelling]

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:38.960
The reason why the opening

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.200
to "A Quiet Place" is so brilliant

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.940
is that it allows the film to go anywhere,

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:47.550
establishing its unique
world, rules, characters,

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.290
and their struggles and traumas

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.640
all in under 10 minutes.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.365
Because the role of an opening

00:05:53.365 --> 00:05:55.248
is not to open just the film,

00:05:55.248 --> 00:05:58.470
but to open its story to
unlimited possibilities

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.616
for its audience to enjoy.

00:06:00.616 --> 00:06:01.524
[gun cocking]

